The sun is low in the west, and the sea beneath it turns from bronze to iron. The wind is warm and salt, with no rain in it yet. The light is going, but slowly; the evening will be long. This is the last boat. She is old and salt-blanched, and she carries more than she was built to carry: refugees and everything they own, bundles and children and cooking pots lashed down between the benches. The storm took the sail days ago, and the sea kept it. Since then everyone rows in turns, and the oars keep their slow rhythm on the water, hour upon hour, patient beyond the patience of any crew. Everyone is tired. The fresh water is nearly gone; there is only what the rains give, and the rains have been few. The food from home is long gone too: the bread went first, weeks ago, then everything else that could keep, and for a long time now everyone has lived on what the fishing lines bring up, raw fish and rain water, the catch of one day against the hunger of the next. There is no land in sight, in any direction. There is no way back. Among the rowers are your three friends: proud and determined Arik, glum and cynical Bearn, and clever Eya. Near you, your fourth friend Agnar sleeps under a tarp, worn hollow by the voyage. You decide not to wake him. Let the sleeping sleep. Your own turn at the oars is over, and Arik's turn has ended too. He is exhausted, but too proud to show it, and keeps rowing, reluctant to hand over the oar. Behind you, the next rowers settle into their seats, and the strokes go on.
> Wait for Arik

Before long, Arik drops down beside you, takes a swallow of rain water from the shared barrel, and looks out west, where the sun is going down. He is not tired. Or he will not show it, which on this boat comes to the same thing. "The gods did not carry us this far to drown us," says Arik. "We rose against the Dragon Emperor knowing what it would cost, and the gods watched us do it. They will not waste us on the sea. There is a land ahead. And when we reach it, it will be ours. We will be the founders of something great. They will tell stories about us." He looks out over the water and does not blink, and there is something in his stillness that demands the land appear. What do you say?
> "Hope will not find water."

Arik is quiet for a moment, weighing it. Then he nods slowly. "Then we plan. First we land. Then we find water and food. Then we build. One thing at a time, until the stories can begin." It is not glory yet. But it is a road that leads there.
> "Do you think the gods sent us out here?"

Arik is quiet. The oars fill the silence with their patient rhythm. "I think they opened a door and pushed us through it," he says. "What we do on the other side is ours. That is the whole point of us."
> Let it rest

Later, Bearn's turn at the oars ends. He sits down heavily beside you and does not speak for a long time. His hands lie open in his lap, and they do not move. The rowing has taken everything; there is nothing left in him, and he makes no pretense otherwise. "I am done," he finally says. "Not tired-done. Done all the way through." He is quiet again. Then he says: "Maybe we should have knelt while we could. The Emperor would have left us our village. Instead we have this boat." He looks at Arik across the deck, at all that undimmed certainty. Then at you. What do you say?
> Say little - just listen

You sit close and say nothing. Bearn does not talk the way Arik talks; words cost him something. So you share the silence instead, the old kind, the kind that does not need filling. After a while he leans back and closes his eyes, and the sounds of the boat go on around you both: oar, rope, sea, breath.
> Sit in silence

Last, Eya's turn at the oars ends. She sits down beside you, pulls a blanket over you both, and looks up at the sky, where the first stars come out between the clouds. "Weed in the water today," says Eya. "Fresh weed, torn off rocks, not old floating stuff. And gulls at noon - gulls do not come this far out unless there is land. I think there is a coast south of us. I cannot promise it." The words settle over the benches. Somewhere behind you, someone repeats them, quietly, to someone else. Hope moves through this boat the same way: one bench at a time. What do you say?
> "If the coast is close, we must not meet it in the dark."

When Eya thinks you are near, she says so, and the oars come in slow, and every eye watches for reefs. Eya nods seriously. "And keep the children away from the rails." The plan is set. Now the sea must cooperate, and the sea has never read a plan.
> "How can you tell?"

"I count the days, and I watch the wind," says Eya. "We have had the wind against us fewer days than it feels. And the sea is different near land: the weed, the birds. I have sailed before. The signs are small, but they are there." She says it plainly, and does not make the signs larger than they are.
> "What if you are wrong?"

"Then we keep rowing," says Eya. "The signs can be wrong. That is why they are signs and not promises. But today I would take a wrong sign over none." She looks at the clouds coming in from the west, and the eyes that count stars are, for a moment, only tired.
> Go to the bow

You go to the bow. The deck is full of sleep now, and your feet learn the way through it, over bundles, past cooking pots, along the dark lane between the benches. Up here the night is wide open, and colder for it. Most of the stars are gone behind cloud, and the wind has changed since sundown, harder, with weather in it. You sit down against the stem and look south, the way Eya said, where a coast might be, and the dark does not give it up. Maybe it is there. Maybe it is close. The boat rows on into the black water, carrying everyone you know, and you keep your eyes forward, because someone might as well.
> Stay at the bow into the night

The night deepens, and the wind hardens, and the boat leans into the work of the water. The last stars go out. The rain comes, a hiss on the sea first, then a hammer on the deck behind you, and the storm arrives at last, in total darkness. People shout. Somewhere behind you a child is crying, and someone is singing to the child, and both sound equally desperate. You stand at the bow and hold the stem and keep your eyes forward, because someone has to, and you gaze into the darkness ahead, black on black, until your eyes learn it. It is almost too late when they learn it: a long line of white water, breaking where no wave should break. The reef. The cry is yours to give, and the whole length of the boat lies between you and the oars, and the sea is already lifting her toward it.
> Stay and try to save the ship

"REEF!" The word tears out of you, and the wind tears it aft, and the storm eats half of it before it passes the mast. A few hear it, and only a few: one calls the reef forward, another asks what, a third cries back water, row her backwards, and the oars muddle against each other, panicked shouting standing in for a plan, confused rowing for an answer. No shout will turn her now. So you try the only thing left at hand. You tear the spare oar from its lashings at the stem and jam it down against the rock, against the whole rising black of it, and you throw everything you have left into the shaft: arms, shoulders, back, a single body's worth of refusal. For a heartbeat the stem comes a hand's width off its line. Then the oar breaks, the recoil flings you down onto the deck, and the rock runs on. Then the reef takes her anyway. The bow rises, hangs, and comes down on the rock with a sound that no boat should make, and the hull tears open beneath your feet, and the sea is aboard all at once. The deck drops away under you, and you are in the black water alone, ahead of everyone, the cries of the boat suddenly far off behind you.
> Fight for the shore alone

You crawl out of the surf onto wet sand and fall down, breathing hard. The sand holds you, solid and unmoving, the first thing in weeks that has not moved beneath you. You never slept, and your body has been running on nothing since the reef, and the sand takes all of you at once. Around you, others stumble out of the dark: mothers calling names into the roar, men dragging bundles, an old man on his knees with both hands pressed to the ground. One of the refugees lies moaning on the sand, badly hurt, and the others gather around. Agnar walks out of the surf unaided, rested, if anyone on this beach is, and steadier than most. Behind you, the wreck settles on the reef, groaning with every wave, refusing even now to finish. And the storm, having broken her, is bringing her in: every wave that lifts her sets her down a little nearer the shore, closer than she has any right to be. Close enough that someone down the beach has found the way out to her, a line of shallower water between the rocks, and figures are wading along it already, hauling their own belongings back through the dark, salvaging while the sea is still too busy to notice. Nothing of yours came ashore with you. Everything you have in the world is still on that boat.
> Search the surf for something to defend yourselves with

You search the surf with numb hands, feeling in the shallows where the wreckage washes. The sea gives up a length of broken oar: its last gift, and a poor one. You wade ashore with it and stand it in the sand above the tide line. A poor weapon, but a weapon.
> See to the children and families

You walk among the families. The children are wrapped in blankets, wet but whole, watching you with wide eyes. A mother touches your hand as you pass, and says nothing, and does not need to. Someone presses your hand between both of theirs, and will not let go until the shaking stops. Nobody says much; the day has taken all the words. But the huddle of people grows warmer wherever you go.
> Sit with the families

The rain goes on, softer now, the storm having spent itself on your arrival. You count the others, and the count comes out: everyone made it ashore. The injured one is being cared for. The wreck groans on the reef behind you, breaking a little more with every wave, but still holding together. There is no going back now, and nowhere else to go. On this beach, tonight, that is not a threat. It is a fact, and facts can be built on. The survivors huddle together on the dark sand in the lee of the dunes, shoulder against shoulder, sharing what warmth the living still carry. Nobody speaks. Agnar sleeps again inside the huddle. The night deepens. The first day ashore waits for morning.


